//RS 
//RS /// Re-raise an already-reported child stop out of a JIT task body, using the
//RS /// carrier its catcher reads and *without* running Rust's panic hook.
//RS ///
//RS /// `jet_scheduler_catch_task_unwind` publishes the child value out of a
//RS /// `String` payload (`jet_scheduler_panic_message`), so the payload type here
//RS /// must match `Prelude/Core.rs`'s `jet_runtime_stop_unwind`, which raises
//RS /// `Box::new(message.to_string())` for the same frame on the AOT tier (I9:
//RS /// engines marshal, they do not re-decide).
//RS ///
//RS /// `resume_unwind` rather than `panic!` is the whole point. The scheduler's
//RS /// process-global hook stays quiet only for `jet_scheduler_converted_unwind`
//RS /// payloads — a cancel/deadline marker, the `__jet_ffi_runtime__: ` marker, or
//RS /// a transported `Stop [` / `Error [` report. A child's own panic message is
//RS /// none of those, so `panic!` here printed a raw `thread '<unnamed>' panicked
//RS /// at Concurrency.rs` banner beside a failure the parent already reports in Jet
//RS /// terms (I2/I4). Widening the quiet set instead would silence genuinely
//RS /// unconvertible host panics, and rendering the report into the payload instead
//RS /// would corrupt the value: `jet_scheduler_panic_message` splits a transported
//RS /// report on `]: `, and an E3001 report carries its source frame, context box
//RS /// and Why/Fix under that header, so `.Panicked(reason)` would carry the whole
//RS /// report instead of the program's own message. `resume_unwind` is also what
//RS /// `jit/runtime_host.rs`'s `runtime_stop_unwind` already uses.
//RS ///
//RS /// #1997: cranelift-jit registers no unwind info, so this must never run with a
//RS /// JIT frame on the stack. It cannot — the callers below raise only after
//RS /// `f()` has returned, from plain Rust frames.
//RS fn resume_child_stop(message: String) -> ! {
//RS     std::panic::resume_unwind(Box::new(message))
//RS }
//RS 
